#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721469 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07ea9294-42cf-5472-81b5-cba6bc6e809f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{3}\\int_{\\pi/2}^{3\\pi/2}\\cos^2t\\sin^2t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721455} {:answer/id #uuid "21028347-fda5-5efc-95e2-a3b11309d582" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}\\int_{\\pi/2}^{3\\pi/2}\\cos^2t\\sin t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721458} {:answer/id #uuid "31a7bac1-d59a-59e9-ab82-f893e89b3b2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}\\int_{\\pi/2}^{3\\pi/2}\\cos^2t\\sin^2t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721460} {:answer/id #uuid "a7c0ef13-e9c8-5a99-a00d-a6cf7d709eb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\sqrt{3}\\int_{\\pi/2}^{3\\pi/2}\\cos^2t\\sin^2t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721470} {:answer/id #uuid "d78b86af-3c95-519e-a913-ce6155f3e624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\sqrt{3}\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}\\cos^2t\\sin^2t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721472}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721460} :answer-field/id #uuid "825c83a4-6215-5d57-a551-86bbaae07990" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8b1f95ea-60ee-5505-99f5-aa88404eefb1" :question/problem "![](images/2f/2f01c4633489a913d93f86ccc83580963ea5cd41bed31347108730a7d60c3ebf.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C x^2y\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the left half of the ellipse $3x^2+y^2=3$, traversed counterclockwise from $(0,\\sqrt{3})$ to $(0,-\\sqrt{3})$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "Use the change of variables formula\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))x'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFirst put the ellipse in standard form:\n\n$$\nx^2+\\frac{y^2}{3}=1.\n$$\n\nIts horizontal semiaxis is $1$ and its vertical semiaxis is $\\sqrt{3}$. Choose\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\cos t,\\sqrt{3}\\sin t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe start, leftmost point, and finish occur at $t=\\pi/2$, $t=\\pi$, and $t=3\\pi/2$, respectively. Thus $\\pi/2\\le t\\le3\\pi/2$ traces the specified left half in the required direction.\n\nSubstitute and differentiate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(\\mathbf r(t))&=(\\cos t)^2(\\sqrt{3}\\sin t)=\\sqrt{3}\\cos^2t\\sin t,\\\\\nx'(t)&=-\\sin t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C x^2y\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\int_{\\pi/2}^{3\\pi/2}\\sqrt{3}\\cos^2t\\sin t(-\\sin t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=-\\sqrt{3}\\int_{\\pi/2}^{3\\pi/2}\\cos^2t\\sin^2t\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlthough $x$ returns to $0$, the integral need not vanish: $x$ decreases on the upper half and increases on the lower half, while $y$ changes sign. The product $x^2y\\,x'(t)$ is nonpositive on both halves. Retain the signed derivative rather than replacing it with speed or $|x'(t)|$."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721586 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "The parametrization follows the specified arc and uses $y\\prime(t)=\\cos t$." :answer/id #uuid "3f8c47c7-0da9-5f8d-b9ef-32ad725d47a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}(4\\cos^2 t+\\sin t)\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721590} {:answer/feedback "This uses $x\\prime(t)=-2\\sin t$, which belongs to $\\mathrm{d}x$." :answer/id #uuid "5411fe20-eca9-5580-aabf-76b009b8743a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}(4\\cos^2 t+\\sin t)\\sin t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721592} {:answer/feedback "The signed derivative is $\\cos t$, which is negative on this interval. Replacing it by its absolute value reverses the sign." :answer/id #uuid "a403504a-c407-52c9-82e8-724e76718d61" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}(4\\cos^2 t+\\sin t)\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721596} {:answer/feedback "These limits follow the lower-right quarter of the ellipse instead of the specified upper-left quarter." :answer/id #uuid "cb84ce19-4bd6-58c2-8dc1-d7985f344bb8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{-\\pi/2}^{0}(4\\cos^2 t+\\sin t)\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721600} {:answer/feedback "The $y$ derivative is missing." :answer/id #uuid "f1158d22-5ac2-5f2a-ab30-17c972c13654" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}(4\\cos^2 t+\\sin t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721603}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721590} :answer-field/id #uuid "150f8f09-5e54-564e-a72f-6c247ee2995c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721585}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "16022ace-9846-544d-a480-0925624f0caa" :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/fa/fa0ea1d3a658c7da952caa60d9a29e19995c5f9aefe138b13dee7ea42c67ab58.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C(x^2+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the upper-left quarter of the ellipse $x^2+4y^2=4$, directed counterclockwise from $A=(0,1)$ to $B=(-2,0)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle x(t),y(t)\\rangle$ that follows the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nWriting the ellipse as $x^2/4+y^2=1$ gives the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle2\\cos t,\\sin t\\rangle.\n$$\n\nThe initial point $(0,1)$ occurs at $t=\\pi/2$, and the terminal point $(-2,0)$ occurs at $t=\\pi$. Increasing $t$ through this interval follows the upper-left arc counterclockwise. Thus\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le t\\le\\pi,\\qquad y'(t)=\\cos t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into $f(x,y)=x^2+y$ gives\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=(2\\cos t)^2+\\sin t=4\\cos^2 t+\\sin t.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\int_C(x^2+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n=\\int_{\\pi/2}^{\\pi}(4\\cos^2 t+\\sin t)\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe factor $\\cos t$ retains its sign: $y$ decreases along this arc."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721598 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "The interval from $0$ to $\\pi$ covers only half of the ellipse." :answer/id #uuid "03198589-4dfb-584d-a382-f291912cc5f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\int_0^{\\pi}(2\\sin t+3\\cos t+1)\\sin t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721584} {:answer/feedback "This follows one clockwise traversal and includes the signed $y$ derivative." :answer/id #uuid "31138b39-3d8e-5766-ba6c-ba23b77ed4ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3\\int_0^{2\\pi}(2\\sin t+3\\cos t+1)\\sin t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721589} {:answer/feedback "The derivative of $3\\cos t$ is $-3\\sin t$, so this expression has the wrong sign." :answer/id #uuid "43679308-f607-534b-bf24-24b1c1edafd5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\int_0^{2\\pi}(2\\sin t+3\\cos t+1)\\sin t\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721591} {:answer/feedback "The factor $y\\prime(t)=-3\\sin t$ is missing." :answer/id #uuid "947e7691-c177-53be-9700-b8ffbcd15a81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_0^{2\\pi}(2\\sin t+3\\cos t+1)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721595} {:answer/feedback "A closed path does not imply that a line integral with a general nonconstant integrand is zero. The endpoint rule for an integral of unity does not apply here." :answer/id #uuid "c87dc130-d048-5885-9182-e498b2977a45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721599}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721589} :answer-field/id #uuid "cb873bf0-19de-5480-8fd2-04db008aa9df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721601}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "b6df7126-e9c7-572e-8458-c423e67e3552" :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/41/416b802710051fa30c2da3f637a8f94fccf8365a39144b6c82553eaad0de40a8.png)\n\nWhich expression is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\oint_C(x+y+1)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the complete ellipse $x^2/4+y^2/9=1$, traversed once clockwise starting at $A=(0,3)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral." :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle x(t),y(t)\\rangle$ that follows the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nChoose a parametrization that begins at the top of the ellipse and initially moves to the right:\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle2\\sin t,3\\cos t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le2\\pi.\n$$\n\nIt lies on the ellipse because $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$. At $t=0$ the point is $(0,3)$; at $t=\\pi/2$ it is $(2,0)$. The path therefore runs clockwise, and the interval $[0,2\\pi]$ covers it exactly once.\n\nThe differential contributes\n\n$$\ny'(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(3\\cos t)=-3\\sin t.\n$$\n\nSubstituting into $f(x,y)=x+y+1$ gives\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=2\\sin t+3\\cos t+1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these expressions, the requested line integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\int_0^{2\\pi}(2\\sin t+3\\cos t+1)(-3\\sin t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nFactoring out $-3$ gives the equivalent definite integral\n\n$$\n-3\\int_0^{2\\pi}(2\\sin t+3\\cos t+1)\\sin t\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nAlthough $C$ is closed, the integrand is not unity. The initial and terminal points alone do not determine this integral."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721602 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/feedback "This uses the derivative of $x(t)$ instead of the derivative of $y(t)$." :answer/id #uuid "20553ffe-b4dd-5b3e-936b-21b837e3eaf6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3\\int_0^1(5t-2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721587} {:answer/feedback "The substitution, limits, and signed $y$ derivative are correct." :answer/id #uuid "30d5a01a-b39e-5991-8199-c01437c7f88a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\int_0^1(5t-2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721588} {:answer/feedback "The factor $y\\prime(t)=2$ is missing." :answer/id #uuid "86e81909-99b7-539e-953f-21b0d540271d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\int_0^1(5t-2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721593} {:answer/feedback "Substituting into $x+y$ gives $5t-2$, not $t-2$." :answer/id #uuid "8d201277-de0a-5272-a1f3-51a15b24dc5e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\int_0^1(t-2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721594} {:answer/feedback "The speed factor belongs to an integral with respect to arc length. Here the differential is $\\mathrm{d}y$." :answer/id #uuid "f682c14e-96e7-5415-abff-02af67df844e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\sqrt{13}\\int_0^1(5t-2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721604}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721588} :answer-field/id #uuid "a828e42e-bb3f-58dd-9bf2-a79935c2c920" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721597}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "cee5807e-01f8-5ab2-8cbb-3e74e1d425f3" :question/problem "![Directed path C](images/e7/e7b1742fad3cea5fb12d4a2e799aae284341f4c818a098f9c4395dbb340fe910.png)\n\nWhich definite integral is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\int_C (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment directed from $A=(-1,-1)$ to $B=(2,1)$, as shown above? Do not evaluate the integral.\n\n*Hint: Use the parametrization $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 3t-1,2t-1\\rangle$.*" :question/worked-solution "For a parametrization $\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle x(t),y(t)\\rangle$ that follows the directed path,\n\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_a^b f(x(t),y(t))\\,y'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nThe hinted parametrization has $\\mathbf r(0)=(-1,-1)$ and $\\mathbf r(1)=(2,1)$, so it follows $C$ for $0\\le t\\le1$. Its $y$ coordinate gives\n\n$$\ny'(t)=\\frac{\\mathrm{d}}{\\mathrm{d}t}(2t-1)=2.\n$$\n\nSubstitute both coordinates into $f(x,y)=x+y$:\n\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=(3t-1)+(2t-1)=5t-2.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C(x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n&=\\int_0^1(5t-2)\\cdot2\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=2\\int_0^1(5t-2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721660 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0b812629-0afc-51b6-b050-0815bc4c6997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac23" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721657} {:answer/id #uuid "3660b365-2f98-52e1-9fef-4c169144ee14" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721662} {:answer/id #uuid "a5f18909-40e2-590e-ab36-b40c8b679f5b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721670} {:answer/id #uuid "ae0322fb-ea51-590e-9d8b-0c09e0aacdb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721672} {:answer/id #uuid "c5874b68-316e-568e-b5a6-dbad31cd9a58" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac43" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721675}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721662} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c860ecc-db13-5627-aa2f-a2f8a17a89ee" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721663}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2d1be979-08c8-5180-9c20-77dd126f6252" :question/problem "![](images/be/be3ad044a611d99e67c89d053d4878a2835be65828a4dc9300697bfd61822994.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C x^2y^3\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the lower-left quarter of the ellipse $4x^2+y^2=4$, traversed from $(-1,0)$ to $(0,-2)$, as shown above.\n" :question/worked-solution "Write the ellipse as $x^2+y^2/4=1$. A parameterization that follows the indicated quarter-ellipse is\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle\\cos t,2\\sin t\\rangle,\\qquad \\pi\\le t\\le\\frac{3\\pi}{2}.\n$$\nAt these bounds the points are $(-1,0)$ and $(0,-2)$, respectively. Use the signed derivative $y'(t)=2\\cos t$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C x^2y^3\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n&=\\int_\\pi^{3\\pi/2}(\\cos^2 t)(2\\sin t)^3(2\\cos t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=16\\int_\\pi^{3\\pi/2}\\sin^3 t\\cos^3 t\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nTo integrate, keep one factor of $\\cos t$ for the differential and use $\\cos^2 t=1-\\sin^2 t$. With $u=\\sin t$ and $\\mathrm{d}u=\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t$, the bounds become $0$ and $-1$:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n16\\int_\\pi^{3\\pi/2}\\sin^3 t\\cos^2 t\\cos t\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n&=16\\int_0^{-1}u^3(1-u^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}u\\\\\n&=16\\left[\\frac{u^4}{4}-\\frac{u^6}{6}\\right]_0^{-1}\\\\\n&=16\\left(\\frac14-\\frac16\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac43.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe positive result is consistent with the direction: $x^2y^3$ is negative inside this arc and $y$ decreases, so the signed contributions are positive.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721667 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1fc0ee97-cd15-530d-9d22-05761f9c041a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721659} {:answer/id #uuid "3614d9b4-e693-5820-9685-bf4ae748f154" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721661} {:answer/id #uuid "79028518-82dc-551c-afd1-0d9e28c3d365" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721668} {:answer/id #uuid "7f56328b-80f1-5639-a3da-8a3a4fb9024e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721669} {:answer/id #uuid "b89218ff-9a2f-55c6-864a-57dec69831d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721674}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721661} :answer-field/id #uuid "c6669d9d-44a5-516d-a077-ee3625f7b3a3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "72368db5-c222-575d-80ae-a0f207460616" :question/problem "![](images/f5/f531f2467284ef84e0af0758a8e8554f1e5f72ea08d30d0b80c83076fbe5d7a3.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C (x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(1,0)$ to $(2,1)$, directed as shown above. Use the parameterization\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 1+t,t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le 1,\n$$\nand the formula\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x=\\int_a^b f(\\mathbf r(t))x'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\n" :question/worked-solution "Here $x(t)=1+t$ and $y(t)=t$, so $x'(t)=1$ and $f(\\mathbf r(t))=1+2t$. The bounds follow the indicated direction: $\\mathbf r(0)=(1,0)$ and $\\mathbf r(1)=(2,1)$.\n\nSubstitute into the supplied formula and evaluate:\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C(x+y)\\,\\mathrm{d}x\n&=\\int_0^1(1+2t)\\cdot1\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=\\left[t+t^2\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\nThe differential is $\\mathrm{d}x=x'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t$, so no arc-length factor is needed.\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721677 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d1b502b-e15f-5dc0-980b-64b8c371671d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{26}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721658} {:answer/id #uuid "5ed8484a-18c7-5138-8931-6f4fd4ca5084" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20\\sqrt{2}}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721664} {:answer/id #uuid "62695905-9dd0-5b58-a4e8-1401ba2dd1a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721665} {:answer/id #uuid "a8f7b70d-3151-5f4c-9e73-1ae63803a1d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{20}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721671} {:answer/id #uuid "b6b4061e-722a-5e50-9da4-c2739805b759" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721673}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721671} :answer-field/id #uuid "698ddfa2-5ec3-5708-9cbb-7d551c0af7b2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721666}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d9961e59-e69e-578b-9ee0-f6415cace784" :question/problem "![](images/09/09a38bb4306728f97b7fecbf9ab1bda7aa957607352c1ab27af09489c271c3ed.png)\n\nEvaluate\n$$\n\\int_C (y^2-x)\\,\\mathrm{d}y,\n$$\nwhere $C$ is the line segment from $(0,1)$ to $(2,3)$, directed as shown above.\n" :question/worked-solution "Parameterize the directed segment by\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)=\\langle 2t,1+2t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n$$\nThis gives the stated initial and terminal points. Because the integral is with respect to $y$, use $y'(t)=2$:\n$$\n\\int_C f(x,y)\\,\\mathrm{d}y=\\int_0^1 f(\\mathbf r(t))y'(t)\\,\\mathrm{d}t.\n$$\nSubstituting both coordinates gives\n$$\nf(\\mathbf r(t))=(1+2t)^2-2t=1+2t+4t^2.\n$$\nTherefore,\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\int_C(y^2-x)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n&=2\\int_0^1(1+2t+4t^2)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\\\\n&=2\\left[t+t^2+\\frac{4t^3}{3}\\right]_0^1\\\\\n&=2\\left(1+1+\\frac43\\right)\\\\\n&=\\frac{20}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721696 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f92bffd-ee6b-5358-a55b-061195c37782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{28}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721692} {:answer/id #uuid "2fa86a5d-d49e-5f57-a6f4-a2764fa42ff4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{72}{5}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721695} {:answer/id #uuid "8c30d3ba-9cc9-58f2-a053-7877ba9b23c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{496}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721703} {:answer/id #uuid "9a4ebef6-dbd9-5403-ab48-227a25bfb152" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{356}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721707} {:answer/id #uuid "d2c99f32-1bde-5667-b660-a3ea498f12a5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{496}{15}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721720}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721695} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc3d11c6-5156-5e34-998a-6bfbd3d1070a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721725}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3f76f6c1-1571-53f4-8523-ff1db6625306" :question/problem "The path $C$ follows $y=x^{2} + 1$ from $A(1,2)$ to $B(2,5)$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(2 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\qquad y(t)=t^{2} + 1.\n$$\n\nThe stated endpoints give $t=1$ at $A$ and $t=2$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives\n\n$$\nx'(t)=1,\\qquad y'(t)=2 t.\n$$\n\nWrite the coefficients of $\\mathrm{d}x$ and $\\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(t)&=\\left(2 \\left(t^{2} + 1\\right)^{2}\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\ &=2 t^{4} + 4 t^{2} + 2\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB(t)&=\\left(- 2 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right) \\\\ &=- 4 t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe differential form uses the sum of these contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(A(t)+B(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(2 t^{4} + 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{2 t^{5}}{5} + 2 t\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{84}{5}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{12}{5}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{72}{5}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721701 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2008fe7b-6696-54e6-ac3e-18bba54c016a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{44}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721694} {:answer/id #uuid "55e816c1-6c2b-5928-990b-d89dcdcdf474" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721697} {:answer/id #uuid "71fb80d2-903c-5bef-9a8c-678a12a97867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721700} {:answer/id #uuid "cb4ab9d1-61d6-5d12-b79b-88eb03c0aafc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{31}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721718} {:answer/id #uuid "ebc3f386-b358-5c83-a3e5-07063c7d96ff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "31" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721723}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721694} :answer-field/id #uuid "8d5c9d0a-ccb7-54cc-9f1e-d6d78636e47c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721705}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "81fe9cf5-37ff-538f-bbf0-170d3e4eacd3" :question/problem "The path $C$ follows $y=\\sqrt{x}$ from $A(1,1)$ to $B(4,2)$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x^{2} - y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 2 x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.\n\n$$\nx(t)=t^{2},\\qquad y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nThe stated endpoints give $t=1$ at $A$ and $t=2$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2 t,\\qquad y'(t)=1.\n$$\n\nWrite the coefficients of $\\mathrm{d}x$ and $\\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(t)&=\\left(\\left(t^{2}\\right)^{2} - \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right) \\\\ &=2 t^{5} - 2 t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB(t)&=\\left(- 2 \\left(t^{2}\\right) + 2 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\ &=- 2 t^{2} + 2 t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe differential form uses the sum of these contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(A(t)+B(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(2 t^{5} - 4 t^{2} + 2 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[\\frac{t^{6}}{3} - \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} + t^{2}\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{44}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{44}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721712 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "84e2bfed-6762-58fc-8e7f-12b1367f021d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721702} {:answer/id #uuid "938bb2f5-feb0-554b-9abc-a97eb3a3d44c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-18" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721706} {:answer/id #uuid "9b147777-ca52-55a2-9d72-9eb958cafddf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{27}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721708} {:answer/id #uuid "bf450041-8a25-5094-ba7e-6afeac50e061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{21}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721714} {:answer/id #uuid "bf9408b6-b338-5e95-a699-28a61bb0a8df" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{27}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721715}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721714} :answer-field/id #uuid "66819af9-0053-50f7-996e-a9922b7d7a03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721699}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ab4240ea-4eb2-5bd9-a42e-70839fd25746" :question/problem "The path $C$ follows $y=x^{2}$ from $A(1,1)$ to $B(2,4)$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- 2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.\n\n$$\nx(t)=t,\\qquad y(t)=t^{2}.\n$$\n\nThe stated endpoints give $t=1$ at $A$ and $t=2$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives\n\n$$\nx'(t)=1,\\qquad y'(t)=2 t.\n$$\n\nWrite the coefficients of $\\mathrm{d}x$ and $\\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(t)&=\\left(- 2 \\left(t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\ &=- 2 t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB(t)&=\\left(- \\left(t^{2}\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right) \\\\ &=- 2 t^{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe differential form uses the sum of these contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(A(t)+B(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(- 2 t^{3} - 2 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[- \\frac{t^{4}}{2} - t^{2}\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(-12\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{3}{2}\\right) \\\\ &=- \\frac{21}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721716 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8c81a8af-d0e3-5bc7-8513-65958c5a74b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{43}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721704} {:answer/id #uuid "a2f3ddd5-14f2-5b35-8d95-7b3caf38c804" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{29}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721709} {:answer/id #uuid "a5651837-b380-5f62-b837-68fe9cf6ef7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721710} {:answer/id #uuid "a88ca6be-d55c-567a-849a-7a90ace940f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{43}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721711} {:answer/id #uuid "ead3ea94-f440-5caf-a685-ca3224262e9d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721722}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721704} :answer-field/id #uuid "d5fdb71d-7741-5a9c-8e88-eb777ae61042" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721721}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "c0177e5b-1c02-520a-94ab-35613c7d02c3" :question/problem "The path $C$ follows $y=2 \\sqrt{x}$ from $A(1,2)$ to $B(4,4)$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- 2 x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(x + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.\n\n$$\nx(t)=t^{2},\\qquad y(t)=2 t.\n$$\n\nThe stated endpoints give $t=1$ at $A$ and $t=2$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives\n\n$$\nx'(t)=2 t,\\qquad y'(t)=2.\n$$\n\nWrite the coefficients of $\\mathrm{d}x$ and $\\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(t)&=\\left(- 2 \\left(t^{2}\\right) + 2 \\left(2 t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right) \\\\ &=- 4 t^{3} + 8 t^{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB(t)&=\\left(\\left(t^{2}\\right) + \\left(2 t\\right)\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\ &=2 t^{2} + 4 t\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe differential form uses the sum of these contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(A(t)+B(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(- 4 t^{3} + 10 t^{2} + 4 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[- t^{4} + \\frac{10 t^{3}}{3} + 2 t^{2}\\right]_{1}^{2} \\\\ &=\\left(\\frac{56}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{13}{3}\\right) \\\\ &=\\frac{43}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721719 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07b8e649-d08b-5401-936e-b3ae04d6306f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{7}{2} + 3 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721691} {:answer/id #uuid "1579afce-5372-5a63-a2b2-82bad98bb49a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5 + 3 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721693} {:answer/id #uuid "61811164-e9fa-5ac8-a097-58d6270324ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{5}{2} + \\frac{3 e}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721698} {:answer/id #uuid "bd52f0a8-a66e-5ed2-b214-3ef276313998" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-7 + 6 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721713} {:answer/id #uuid "ed1c0d63-2bfa-5913-b05c-1fa1dd823034" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-4 + 3 e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721724}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721724} :answer-field/id #uuid "c03b7adc-808a-5819-aa42-141b07cdb89c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721717}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "cd4305eb-3db4-5d4a-9b39-99606f708df9" :question/problem "The path $C$ follows $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ from $A(0,0)$ to $B(1,1)$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(4 y e^{x y}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(-1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Choose a coordinate parameter that keeps the curve smooth.\n\n$$\nx(t)=t^{3},\\qquad y(t)=t.\n$$\n\nThe stated endpoints give $t=0$ at $A$ and $t=1$ at $B$; retain that order in the integral. Differentiation gives\n\n$$\nx'(t)=3 t^{2},\\qquad y'(t)=1.\n$$\n\nWrite the coefficients of $\\mathrm{d}x$ and $\\mathrm{d}y$ as $P$ and $Q$. Substitute both coordinates and multiply by the corresponding derivative. Denote the two contributions by $A(t)$ and $B(t)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nA(t)&=\\left(4 \\left(t\\right) e^{\\left(t^{3}\\right) \\left(t\\right)}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 t^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=\\left(4 t e^{t^{4}}\\right)\\cdot \\left(3 t^{2}\\right) \\\\ &=12 t^{3} e^{t^{4}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nB(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\ &=-1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe differential form uses the sum of these contributions:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(A(t)+B(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(12 t^{3} e^{t^{4}} - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ &=\\left[- t + 3 e^{t^{4}}\\right]_{0}^{1} \\\\ &=\\left(-1 + 3 e\\right)-\\left(3\\right) \\\\ &=-4 + 3 e\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721734 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ac5395c-c2b3-5b08-8023-aaca1ee793b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "12 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721727} {:answer/id #uuid "15136879-3b49-56e4-97b9-6a240c578351" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 8 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721730} {:answer/id #uuid "9255fce6-0b03-5c13-9bcf-5f965055b4a3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 4 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721746} {:answer/id #uuid "9a529a78-d1e5-5eb4-b241-dd833c8cfac1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721749} {:answer/id #uuid "fb428f15-f4a4-5389-bbf3-df65dae645ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 2 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721760}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721746} :answer-field/id #uuid "882b94f2-cc65-5fb1-a93f-bec2cd65e246" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "37e55ce8-4fee-56f1-a501-d422cebf7840" :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(- x - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(2 x - y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the ellipse $4 x^{2} + y^{2}=4$, traversed clockwise from $\\left(1,0\\right)$ to $\\left(-1,0\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "Use the parametrization $x=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$, $y=2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$. The endpoints and clockwise traversal give $t$ from $2 \\pi$ to $\\pi$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{d}x &=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ \\mathrm{d}y &=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates and their signed differentials. Let $U$ be the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}x$ term and $V$ the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}y$ term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(t) &=\\left(- 4 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ V(t) &=\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{2 \\pi}^{\\pi}[U(t)+V(t)]\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$, combine the terms to obtain\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{2 \\pi}^{\\pi}\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nAn antiderivative of the displayed integrand is $F(t)=4 t - \\frac{3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{2}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I &= \\left[4 t - \\frac{3 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}}{2}\\right]_{2 \\pi}^{\\pi} \\\\ &= \\left(4 \\pi\\right)-\\left(8 \\pi\\right) \\\\ &= - 4 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721748 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "78ece0e2-abee-52a7-80d3-08f033269146" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 6 \\pi - 16" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721738} {:answer/id #uuid "7c220af8-75a0-541b-aeb3-d0eff1f74ad0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721739} {:answer/id #uuid "7d8899ea-be45-50d3-b95a-b84005a2fee2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721740} {:answer/id #uuid "de2aa394-489b-5399-8709-e183f7a51565" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8 + \\frac{9 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721755} {:answer/id #uuid "e30d30b8-9e87-5be1-b379-490d0185eded" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-8 - \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721757}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721739} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee246096-f616-5ef5-8155-a06c14c92362" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721758}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "99de5607-77d1-5488-8f20-571650098acd" :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(2 x + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the ellipse $x^{2} + 9 y^{2}=9$, traversed clockwise from $\\left(0,-1\\right)$ to $\\left(0,1\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "Use the parametrization $x=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$, $y=\\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$. The endpoints and clockwise traversal give $t$ from $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ to $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{d}x &=- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ \\mathrm{d}y &=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates and their signed differentials. Let $U$ be the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}x$ term and $V$ the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}y$ term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(t) &=\\left(\\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 6 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ V(t) &=\\left(2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}[U(t)+V(t)]\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$, combine the terms to obtain\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\left(- 16 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nAn antiderivative of the displayed integrand is $F(t)=- 3 t - 8 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I &= \\left[- 3 t - 8 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}} \\\\ &= \\left(-8 - \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{9 \\pi}{2} - 8\\right) \\\\ &= 3 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721751 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14403629-e2c6-58d4-acab-48d8ae0bd880" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11 + 9 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721729} {:answer/id #uuid "17713785-26d2-5124-9404-3f046fd0a845" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 21 \\pi - 11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721731} {:answer/id #uuid "83501348-16af-5f4b-85fc-4f0d1e2d2377" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 12 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721741} {:answer/id #uuid "89a3576d-7d01-5b23-9b62-49b93bf9c0c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11 - 3 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721744} {:answer/id #uuid "e2d570f3-b868-5bd9-a429-d118959afc40" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{2} - \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721756}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721744} :answer-field/id #uuid "58301988-efb4-525a-857f-a35f30782a15" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721736}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "bee5c554-afb9-526b-b047-3d31cab5f13d" :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(x + y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x - 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the ellipse $9 x^{2} + 4 y^{2}=36$, traversed counterclockwise from $\\left(0,-3\\right)$ to $\\left(2,0\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "Use the parametrization $x=2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$, $y=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$. The endpoints and counterclockwise traversal give $t$ from $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ to $2 \\pi$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{d}x &=- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ \\mathrm{d}y &=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates and their signed differentials. Let $U$ be the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}x$ term and $V$ the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}y$ term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(t) &=\\left(3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- 2 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ V(t) &=\\left(- 6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}[U(t)+V(t)]\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$, combine the terms to obtain\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi}\\left(- 22 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 6\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nAn antiderivative of the displayed integrand is $F(t)=- 6 t - 11 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I &= \\left[- 6 t - 11 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{2 \\pi} \\\\ &= \\left(- 12 \\pi\\right)-\\left(- 9 \\pi - 11\\right) \\\\ &= 11 - 3 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721754 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "103189c1-8a8f-5b70-b3d6-832b004409c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20 - 24 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721728} {:answer/id #uuid "1a132e5e-7716-5ac8-935c-a653837a235f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 6 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721732} {:answer/id #uuid "2b111408-c2c8-59c0-8c71-ed1f413b916d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10 + 9 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721733} {:answer/id #uuid "88e18ebf-87f3-5c40-8918-eef4de9fa341" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- 3 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721743} {:answer/id #uuid "a1080015-75fb-59b0-8fd3-2c1b5db16b7f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10 - 15 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721750}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721732} :answer-field/id #uuid "f17c02d9-f4db-51d8-9d1b-c9e33dd0669b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721759}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d7c8f35e-d405-53ba-9744-f17206902dee" :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(- 2 x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 2 x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the ellipse $9 x^{2} + y^{2}=9$, traversed counterclockwise from $\\left(0,-3\\right)$ to $\\left(0,3\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "Use the parametrization $x=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$, $y=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$. The endpoints and counterclockwise traversal give $t$ from $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$ to $\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{d}x &=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ \\mathrm{d}y &=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates and their signed differentials. Let $U$ be the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}x$ term and $V$ the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}y$ term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(t) &=\\left(6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ V(t) &=\\left(6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}}[U(t)+V(t)]\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$, combine the terms to obtain\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}}\\left(20 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} - 6\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nAn antiderivative of the displayed integrand is $F(t)=- 6 t + 10 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I &= \\left[- 6 t + 10 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{5 \\pi}{2}} \\\\ &= \\left(10 - 15 \\pi\\right)-\\left(10 - 9 \\pi\\right) \\\\ &= - 6 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721761 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "56d966cd-a9ec-5dbf-8e6a-33e8301dee3e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721735} {:answer/id #uuid "7857fa6e-6407-5f00-bd8a-d717ac950686" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3 \\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721737} {:answer/id #uuid "8fa29981-9d93-579f-8ccc-0409576be815" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "10 + \\frac{9 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721745} {:answer/id #uuid "957008a4-22d1-5144-b537-7f737ef7b92f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-10 - \\frac{3 \\pi}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721747} {:answer/id #uuid "d70b1bb9-3110-5d23-a882-e6e7e6745a43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6 \\pi + 20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721753}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721737} :answer-field/id #uuid "c319cd4c-6c98-50a6-8b0e-f91a1faf8c03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721752}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fdbd82ff-8d0b-59c0-b582-82eb1fc61fb2" :question/problem "Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C\\left(- 2 x - y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(x + 2 y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is the arc of the ellipse $9 x^{2} + y^{2}=9$, traversed counterclockwise from $\\left(0,3\\right)$ to $\\left(0,-3\\right)$." :question/worked-solution "Use the parametrization $x=\\cos{\\left(t \\right)}$, $y=3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}$. The endpoints and counterclockwise traversal give $t$ from $\\frac{\\pi}{2}$ to $\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mathrm{d}x &=- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\ \\mathrm{d}y &=3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}t\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstitute the coordinates and their signed differentials. Let $U$ be the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}x$ term and $V$ the contribution from the $\\mathrm{d}y$ term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}U(t) &=\\left(- 3 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} - 2 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(- \\sin{\\left(t \\right)}\\right) \\\\ V(t) &=\\left(6 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} + \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\left(3 \\cos{\\left(t \\right)}\\right)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}[U(t)+V(t)]\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nUsing $\\sin^2t+\\cos^2t=1$, combine the terms to obtain\n\n$$\nI=\\int_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}}\\left(20 \\sin{\\left(t \\right)} \\cos{\\left(t \\right)} + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t\n$$\n\nAn antiderivative of the displayed integrand is $F(t)=3 t + 10 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}$. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}I &= \\left[3 t + 10 \\sin^{2}{\\left(t \\right)}\\right]_{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{3 \\pi}{2}} \\\\ &= \\left(10 + \\frac{9 \\pi}{2}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{3 \\pi}{2} + 10\\right) \\\\ &= 3 \\pi\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721772 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2b81011c-7bb5-5ab4-a7ba-ebfeb35a21f7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721769} {:answer/id #uuid "34496330-b454-5b47-aabc-32973d958609" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{112}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721771} {:answer/id #uuid "4507c708-a57a-5ce0-b69e-c52296f5468b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721774} {:answer/id #uuid "5b7181cd-c0ea-5040-98cd-eebbf940deb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721776} {:answer/id #uuid "bc8e9890-350c-57c6-aa57-e07f1efa9867" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{52}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721791}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721774} :answer-field/id #uuid "da2e003f-7deb-591d-a9f5-1b73100f2c8d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721793}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "37112010-4a76-5fb8-9973-3bf783610679" :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(2,2)$ to $B(0,4)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- x^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(2 y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle 2,2\\rangle+t\\langle -2,2\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle 2 - 2 t,2 t + 2\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=2 - 2 t$, $y(t)=2 t + 2$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle -2,2\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=- x^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=2 y^{2}$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 - 2 t\\right)^{2}\\cdot \\left(-2\\right) \\\\\n&=8 t^{2} - 16 t + 8, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t + 2\\right)^{2}\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=16 t^{2} + 32 t + 16.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(24 t^{2} + 16 t + 24\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[8 t^{3} + 8 t^{2} + 24 t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=8 t^{3} + 8 t^{2} + 24 t, \\\\\nF(1)&=8 \\left(1\\right)^{3} + 8 \\left(1\\right)^{2} + 24 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=40, \\\\\nF(0)&=8 \\left(0\\right)^{3} + 8 \\left(0\\right)^{2} + 24 \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(40\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=40.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721778 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01565bc1-c4f3-55fb-ab09-1fce1b7d731b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721763} {:answer/id #uuid "a3702c4f-42bc-5fd3-9088-a400159a2617" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721786} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c04ede-bade-50d8-985e-e2a9cd67c8d7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721787} {:answer/id #uuid "e609b430-e25b-58a9-83b3-d8a30e1f7588" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721795} {:answer/id #uuid "ea15da88-a8a8-53a0-90e8-117880ece1e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721796}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721786} :answer-field/id #uuid "975d6a12-c2cc-5a3e-86ff-754f054d320a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721784}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6a58b6e4-3cb5-54e4-9058-e7af85a05164" :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(0,-2)$ to $B(1,0)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle 0,-2\\rangle+t\\langle 1,2\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle t,2 t - 2\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=t$, $y(t)=2 t - 2$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 1,2\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=- x$ and $Q(x,y)=y$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(t\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=- t, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t - 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=4 t - 4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(3 t - 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2} - 4 t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2} - 4 t, \\\\\nF(1)&=\\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2} - 4 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{5}{2}, \\\\\nF(0)&=\\frac{3 \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2} - 4 \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{5}{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{5}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721780 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "11df0b2b-8201-5ad9-8b7f-de927988c41a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721766} {:answer/id #uuid "162c6927-96cd-5caa-801d-f9dc24da4191" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721767} {:answer/id #uuid "373a6238-147a-5e5f-b538-ce7a2bb9a1fd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721773} {:answer/id #uuid "6b2f9866-2307-5c98-872c-5535a1447abd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721779} {:answer/id #uuid "aa603ea3-522c-5fb5-859b-99c5de83bd16" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{8}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721789}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721767} :answer-field/id #uuid "eb8be0a9-5f77-501d-a892-ad682add2508" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721797}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6e9f0fae-a7cc-50f5-a553-b333e6110a81" :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(1,2)$ to $B(0,1)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- y^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle 1,2\\rangle+t\\langle -1,-1\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle 1 - t,2 - t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=1 - t$, $y(t)=2 - t$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle -1,-1\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=x^{2}$ and $Q(x,y)=- y^{2}$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1 - t\\right)^{2}\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=- t^{2} + 2 t - 1, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 - t\\right)^{2}\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=t^{2} - 4 t + 4.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(3 - 2 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- t^{2} + 3 t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- t^{2} + 3 t, \\\\\nF(1)&=- \\left(1\\right)^{2} + 3 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=2, \\\\\nF(0)&=- \\left(0\\right)^{2} + 3 \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(2\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721790 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0cafa2f4-acb2-5545-a3f5-3bee0ea7abae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721765} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8e6b38-935d-5403-8e69-16945242c159" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721768} {:answer/id #uuid "2d63d8d8-2f6e-5784-bd3b-dfe55d23dd10" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721770} {:answer/id #uuid "68a0697f-e1d0-56d0-9d9e-c6ede3dfec34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721777} {:answer/id #uuid "a32bab91-d825-5416-86d5-d00be040a606" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721785}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721768} :answer-field/id #uuid "941569fc-bb30-56b1-b238-777435beee2e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721783}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "b5a0435c-758d-52f3-9402-ca42622c8edd" :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(-1,-1)$ to $B(-2,0)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle -1,-1\\rangle+t\\langle -1,1\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle - t - 1,t - 1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=- t - 1$, $y(t)=t - 1$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle -1,1\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=2 x$ and $Q(x,y)=- y$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(2\\right)\\cdot \\left(- t - 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=2 t + 2, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(t - 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=1 - t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(t + 3\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{t^{2}}{2} + 3 t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=\\frac{t^{2}}{2} + 3 t, \\\\\nF(1)&=\\frac{\\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2} + 3 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{7}{2}, \\\\\nF(0)&=\\frac{\\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2} + 3 \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{7}{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{7}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721792 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0a4f34b8-a172-5367-95b8-f5119a4bf459" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721764} {:answer/id #uuid "48919cd5-b17d-5797-8363-c85b922b0cc6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721775} {:answer/id #uuid "7e4e2883-967f-57d1-8a9b-f0ddc8d7fa6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721781} {:answer/id #uuid "a661ed14-7f2c-58e1-9627-63e52e4fb8d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721788} {:answer/id #uuid "dcbb56ed-42be-5e1d-aacc-f280b2f14a2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721794}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721794} :answer-field/id #uuid "85d3cf09-c581-51f7-8168-5eaeea63f65e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721782}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "d3fbc752-990d-56f5-ad7b-eb8e0b3ababc" :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(-1,-2)$ to $B(0,-3)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- 2 x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle -1,-2\\rangle+t\\langle 1,-1\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle t - 1,- t - 2\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=t - 1$, $y(t)=- t - 2$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 1,-1\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=- y$ and $Q(x,y)=- 2 x$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(- t - 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=t + 2, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-2\\right)\\cdot \\left(t - 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=2 t - 2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(3 t\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=\\frac{3 t^{2}}{2}, \\\\\nF(1)&=\\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2} \\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}, \\\\\nF(0)&=\\frac{3 \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2} \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721804 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "25d06d5e-259a-5d83-aca0-b007f248fce6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{40}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721803} {:answer/id #uuid "3fc07d0c-0950-5e66-8cd0-7e3d659d74f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{55}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721810} {:answer/id #uuid "4c7a4cc7-aee7-5feb-aecd-a8f94f6434c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{31}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721812} {:answer/id #uuid "51cfaa06-83f1-548e-8aaf-1faada2e6fc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{49}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721813} {:answer/id #uuid "f6bcd97d-118c-5049-aa90-628b47e260b4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{37}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721823}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721812} :answer-field/id #uuid "3bc47f2b-708a-53f2-bacb-8b9f60903e10" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721807}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "2606986b-3bce-5fce-a65a-9ce1baa2d885" :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(-3,1)$ to $B(-4,3)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Use the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\langle - t - 2,2 t - 1\\rangle,\n$$\n\nwith parameter bounds chosen to match this traversal, to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Match the parameter to the ordered endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(1)&=\\langle -3,1\\rangle=A, \\\\\n\\mathbf{r}(2)&=\\langle -4,3\\rangle=B.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe traversal therefore takes $t$ from $1$ to $2$. The order of these bounds records the direction.\n\nHere $x(t)=- t - 2$, $y(t)=2 t - 1$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle -1,2\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=- x y$ and $Q(x,y)=1$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(- t - 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t - 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=- 2 t^{2} - 3 t + 2, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(- 2 t^{2} - 3 t + 4\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{2 t^{3}}{3} - \\frac{3 t^{2}}{2} + 4 t\\right]_{1}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- \\frac{2 t^{3}}{3} - \\frac{3 t^{2}}{2} + 4 t, \\\\\nF(2)&=- \\frac{2 \\left(2\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{3 \\left(2\\right)^{2}}{2} + 4 \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{10}{3}, \\\\\nF(1)&=- \\frac{2 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2} + 4 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{11}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(2)-F(1) \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{10}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{11}{6}\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{31}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe ordered bounds already enforce the stated traversal; no extra sign reversal is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721808 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "112caa6a-b0c7-578a-b475-5bf1b7909aa4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{80}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721800} {:answer/id #uuid "a42578f5-8623-5def-815b-26aeed103997" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{74}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721816} {:answer/id #uuid "a8a02b32-a2a5-52b8-8b35-c241b04e81f4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{74}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721817} {:answer/id #uuid "bd170661-69ad-547b-8c86-a51cf33900d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{86}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721819} {:answer/id #uuid "f16bf9a6-4a54-5b5d-bfa5-b8228651acaf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{86}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721821}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721821} :answer-field/id #uuid "3d22161b-1bbe-54e2-91a8-dc7c271c1c55" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721809}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "3c11ae14-db41-5e2f-b9f6-9f1dd658fafd" :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(4,4)$ to $B(0,2)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Use the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\langle 2 t,t + 2\\rangle,\n$$\n\nwith parameter bounds chosen to match this traversal, to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(-1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Match the parameter to the ordered endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(2)&=\\langle 4,4\\rangle=A, \\\\\n\\mathbf{r}(0)&=\\langle 0,2\\rangle=B.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe traversal therefore takes $t$ from $2$ to $0$. The order of these bounds records the direction.\n\nHere $x(t)=2 t$, $y(t)=t + 2$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 2,1\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=- x y$ and $Q(x,y)=-1$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(t + 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=- 4 t^{2} - 8 t, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=-1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{2}^{0}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{2}^{0}\\left(- 4 t^{2} - 8 t - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} - 4 t^{2} - t\\right]_{2}^{0}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} - 4 t^{2} - t, \\\\\nF(0)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3} - 4 \\left(0\\right)^{2} - \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0, \\\\\nF(2)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(2\\right)^{3}}{3} - 4 \\left(2\\right)^{2} - \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{86}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(0)-F(2) \\\\\n&=\\left(0\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{86}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{86}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe ordered bounds already enforce the stated traversal; no extra sign reversal is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721825 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03630c98-449d-59ff-b862-b7219a3b6955" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721799} {:answer/id #uuid "2179b83e-c0f4-54bc-a1e5-b87b4f440e1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721801} {:answer/id #uuid "2182f909-cf98-5300-94c1-9e67f1d09d08" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721802} {:answer/id #uuid "8c9c929e-c976-5ac3-a9b5-8f8253c38995" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{17}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721815} {:answer/id #uuid "b77d3845-a551-5abb-9d6b-3e212d3cc5ee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721818}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721815} :answer-field/id #uuid "4879c0d3-29fe-56bb-90e5-1bd02027bea6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721811}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fd78aa20-adb5-5a72-99a4-501498c934c9" :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(1,-4)$ to $B(2,-6)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Use the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\langle t,- 2 t - 2\\rangle,\n$$\n\nwith parameter bounds chosen to match this traversal, to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(-1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Match the parameter to the ordered endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(1)&=\\langle 1,-4\\rangle=A, \\\\\n\\mathbf{r}(2)&=\\langle 2,-6\\rangle=B.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe traversal therefore takes $t$ from $1$ to $2$. The order of these bounds records the direction.\n\nHere $x(t)=t$, $y(t)=- 2 t - 2$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 1,-2\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=x y$ and $Q(x,y)=-1$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(t\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 t - 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=- 2 t^{2} - 2 t, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-2\\right) \\\\\n&=2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{2}\\left(- 2 t^{2} - 2 t + 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{2 t^{3}}{3} - t^{2} + 2 t\\right]_{1}^{2}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- \\frac{2 t^{3}}{3} - t^{2} + 2 t, \\\\\nF(2)&=- \\frac{2 \\left(2\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\left(2\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{16}{3}, \\\\\nF(1)&=- \\frac{2 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\left(1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(2)-F(1) \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{16}{3}\\right)-\\left(\\frac{1}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{17}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe ordered bounds already enforce the stated traversal; no extra sign reversal is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721826 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33e4a935-42dc-5ed2-932e-5bc63ca3a061" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721805} {:answer/id #uuid "3439a253-885f-554e-999c-7e955025d9c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721806} {:answer/id #uuid "71bdc6c1-5098-58ce-93b5-060d0614a499" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721814} {:answer/id #uuid "f45bb79a-fb1a-5846-8a4c-0b7c3d4cdae4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721822} {:answer/id #uuid "fc6df836-cba1-5a44-9cb7-0fd2f25f7f11" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721824}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721814} :answer-field/id #uuid "e1e91de3-30d7-5103-8d74-920926c8f0ca" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721820}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "fe25d7bf-5480-5f34-8381-dc8d833dfeca" :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(-2,-1)$ to $B(2,3)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Use the parametrization\n\n$$\n\\mathbf{r}(t)=\\langle - 2 t,1 - 2 t\\rangle,\n$$\n\nwith parameter bounds chosen to match this traversal, to evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(- y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Match the parameter to the ordered endpoints:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(1)&=\\langle -2,-1\\rangle=A, \\\\\n\\mathbf{r}(-1)&=\\langle 2,3\\rangle=B.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe traversal therefore takes $t$ from $1$ to $-1$. The order of these bounds records the direction.\n\nHere $x(t)=- 2 t$, $y(t)=1 - 2 t$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle -2,-2\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=- y$ and $Q(x,y)=x y$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1 - 2 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(-2\\right) \\\\\n&=2 - 4 t, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(- 2 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(1 - 2 t\\right)\\cdot \\left(-2\\right) \\\\\n&=- 8 t^{2} + 4 t.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{1}^{-1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{1}^{-1}\\left(2 - 8 t^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{8 t^{3}}{3} + 2 t\\right]_{1}^{-1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- \\frac{8 t^{3}}{3} + 2 t, \\\\\nF(-1)&=- \\frac{8 \\left(-1\\right)^{3}}{3} + 2 \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\\nF(1)&=- \\frac{8 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} + 2 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{2}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(-1)-F(1) \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)-\\left(- \\frac{2}{3}\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{4}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe ordered bounds already enforce the stated traversal; no extra sign reversal is needed."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721829 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0181b82c-59de-56f7-ab58-82ddaaa30259" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721827} {:answer/id #uuid "14b5a170-a6f3-5e0e-9da3-d35c3fb76841" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{13}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721831} {:answer/id #uuid "76addb11-ee9e-5aec-8dce-80e348c268c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721846} {:answer/id #uuid "8857047b-b686-591e-b47d-e5f185a01bc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{16}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721849} {:answer/id #uuid "a539007e-bff5-549f-89c3-2eccdd09e3fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{19}{6}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721851}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721846} :answer-field/id #uuid "1d65e4bb-e66a-5c8e-95e3-15d859fdd770" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721833}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "06422b75-a347-5e9b-9800-8bfa33bd6cd6" :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(1,1)$ to $B(2,3)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(-1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle 1,1\\rangle+t\\langle 1,2\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle t + 1,2 t + 1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=t + 1$, $y(t)=2 t + 1$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 1,2\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=x y$ and $Q(x,y)=-1$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(t + 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t + 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=2 t^{2} + 3 t + 1, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=-2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(2 t^{2} + 3 t - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[\\frac{2 t^{3}}{3} + \\frac{3 t^{2}}{2} - t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=\\frac{2 t^{3}}{3} + \\frac{3 t^{2}}{2} - t, \\\\\nF(1)&=\\frac{2 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} + \\frac{3 \\left(1\\right)^{2}}{2} - \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{7}{6}, \\\\\nF(0)&=\\frac{2 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3} + \\frac{3 \\left(0\\right)^{2}}{2} - \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{7}{6}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{7}{6}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721832 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0d240176-41fb-5e42-8e81-54d63c972c8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{10}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721830} {:answer/id #uuid "4e19802a-5d97-5db8-b628-c82ffb0d03bd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721838} {:answer/id #uuid "5a686aed-37bd-5dfe-9106-7e47a38dc788" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721841} {:answer/id #uuid "82b9ac8c-7238-5acb-8817-71993b497021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{7}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721848} {:answer/id #uuid "bb0bb7d3-c654-505a-8588-e7abb68a8a75" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{11}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721854}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721854} :answer-field/id #uuid "4a30b126-b397-5574-8cb8-d50cf67e1ab8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721837}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1afe0800-d297-570b-a6d8-2a5ef80f4cfb" :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(2,1)$ to $B(4,0)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(-1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle 2,1\\rangle+t\\langle 2,-1\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle 2 t + 2,1 - t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=2 t + 2$, $y(t)=1 - t$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 2,-1\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=x y$ and $Q(x,y)=-1$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t + 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(1 - t\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=4 - 4 t^{2}, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(5 - 4 t^{2}\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} + 5 t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} + 5 t, \\\\\nF(1)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} + 5 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{11}{3}, \\\\\nF(0)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3} + 5 \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{11}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{11}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721834 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "03f134b9-a906-5e1d-87f3-787d5fc33b82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721828} {:answer/id #uuid "5918fb55-7f48-5adf-be41-266311ac17ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721840} {:answer/id #uuid "631ac59e-1cdb-5ce8-81aa-462aab17be86" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{2}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721842} {:answer/id #uuid "657bf408-68b4-58bb-b036-d481d3783a02" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721844} {:answer/id #uuid "b2abfd62-2f10-5e03-9109-a077f816db3a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721853}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721840} :answer-field/id #uuid "404d4e44-d862-5329-97e8-a16344e75cdb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721836}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1f8ed101-4079-58ea-ac55-67e21c280bd1" :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(-1,-1)$ to $B(0,1)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle -1,-1\\rangle+t\\langle 1,2\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle t - 1,2 t - 1\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=t - 1$, $y(t)=2 t - 1$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 1,2\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=1$ and $Q(x,y)=- x y$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=1, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(t - 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t - 1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=- 4 t^{2} + 6 t - 2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- 4 t^{2} + 6 t - 1\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} + 3 t^{2} - t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} + 3 t^{2} - t, \\\\\nF(1)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} + 3 \\left(1\\right)^{2} - \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{2}{3}, \\\\\nF(0)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3} + 3 \\left(0\\right)^{2} - \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(\\frac{2}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=\\frac{2}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721839 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3b62b964-e4b3-581a-909d-f6f30a3b7849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721835} {:answer/id #uuid "63cb10ae-ac14-5f0f-a3c6-5d3e33a6fcf9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{19}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721843} {:answer/id #uuid "6b505998-1c62-5e1c-8415-ce12de8bc73d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{1}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721845} {:answer/id #uuid "a10112ad-69b5-5019-9fd5-ae304bf36774" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "- \\frac{13}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721850} {:answer/id #uuid "b0d6124b-e841-5dc5-8fa2-bb56f2873a9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "\\frac{4}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721852}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186721845} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a0de7cd-2198-528e-b82f-4003563ca408" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186721847}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4e8eb9a4-1aba-5a66-a9f3-becb1b0b1ae5" :question/problem "The path $C$ is the line segment from $A(2,0)$ to $B(4,-1)$, traversed from $A$ to $B$. Evaluate\n\n$$\n\\int_C \\left(x y\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}x+\\left(- x\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}y.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Use the endpoint parametrization:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n\\mathbf{r}(t)&=\\mathbf{a}+t(\\mathbf{b}-\\mathbf{a}) \\\\\n&=\\langle 2,0\\rangle+t\\langle 2,-1\\rangle \\\\\n&=\\langle 2 t + 2,- t\\rangle,\\qquad 0\\le t\\le1.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere $x(t)=2 t + 2$, $y(t)=- t$, and $\\mathbf{r}'(t)=\\langle 2,-1\\rangle$. Thus $\\mathrm{d}x=2\\,\\mathrm{d}t$ and $\\mathrm{d}y=-1\\,\\mathrm{d}t$.\n\nWrite $P(x,y)=x y$ and $Q(x,y)=- x$. Evaluate $P$ and $Q$ along the segment and retain their corresponding signed differentials:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nP\\,x'(t)&=\\left(1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t + 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(- t\\right)\\cdot \\left(2\\right) \\\\\n&=- 4 t^{2} - 4 t, \\\\\nQ\\,y'(t)&=\\left(-1\\right)\\cdot \\left(2 t + 2\\right)\\cdot \\left(-1\\right) \\\\\n&=2 t + 2.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $I$ denote the requested integral. Using the power rule gives an antiderivative $F(t)$ and the endpoint evaluation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(P\\,x'(t)+Q\\,y'(t)\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\int_{0}^{1}\\left(- 4 t^{2} - 2 t + 2\\right)\\,\\mathrm{d}t \\\\\n&=\\left[- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} - t^{2} + 2 t\\right]_{0}^{1}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nF(t)&=- \\frac{4 t^{3}}{3} - t^{2} + 2 t, \\\\\nF(1)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(1\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\left(1\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(1\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{1}{3}, \\\\\nF(0)&=- \\frac{4 \\left(0\\right)^{3}}{3} - \\left(0\\right)^{2} + 2 \\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=0.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nI&=F(1)-F(0) \\\\\n&=\\left(- \\frac{1}{3}\\right)-\\left(0\\right) \\\\\n&=- \\frac{1}{3}.\n\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
